<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibiranu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibiranu was king of Ugarit in the later thirteenth century BCE, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammishtamru-ii">Ammishtamru II</a>. Letters from the Hittite court reproached him for failing to pay homage in person and for neglecting his military obligations, signs of growing strain between Ugarit and its overlord. He was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niqmaddu-iii">Niqmaddu III</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ibiranu was the sixth king of Ugarit, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> in northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and reigned for about a decade, from around 1235 BCE to around 1225 BCE or a few years later. He was a contemporary of the Hittite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudhaliya-iv">Tudhaliya IV</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatti">Hatti</a>. As ruler of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state of Hatti he answered to the Hittite viceroy at Carchemish, who oversaw the Syrian vassals on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-king">Great King</a>'s behalf. In 1956 the scholar Gordon suggested that his name meant horsemen, formed from ibr and the suffix -ân.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Three documents show the strain in his dealings with his overlord. In a letter catalogued as RS 17.247, the Hittite prince and official Piḫa-walwi rebuked him on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-king">Great King</a>'s behalf, asking why he had not come before His Majesty since taking the throne, nor even sent messengers, and warning that His Majesty was very angry.</p><p>When Ibiranu did send gifts, an Ugaritic ambassador stationed at Ḫattuša wrote to him in RS 17.383 that the Hittite court was unhappy with the gems he had chosen and urged him to send lapis lazuli to appease the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-king">Great King</a>. A third text, RS 17.59, is a decree of Ini-Teššub, the Viceroy of Carchemish, answering Ibiranu's reluctance to supply infantry and chariotry for imperial campaigns: Ini-Teššub in the end allowed him to pay a heavy tax in place of sending troops.</p><p>Historians read these delays, evasions and acts of neglect as signs of the waning power of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> to compel its Levantine vassals, and of the friction between imperial demands and local autonomy that continued under his successor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ibiranu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ibiranu</b>, Niqmaddu III and Ammurapi, the last king, ruled in the troubled late thirteenth and early twelfth centuries BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ugarit">Kingdom of Ugarit · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
